{
  "book": "1. Electricity, Magnetism.pdf",
  "pages": 10,
  "source_grounded": true,
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "U01",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Electric charge",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how_measure"
      ],
      "text": "Positive and negative electric charges; charge is expressed with q and measured in coulombs; q = ne is given.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p1:L16-L24"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U02",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Electric field",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "where"
      ],
      "text": "The source describes a small area around electric charges where their effect can be felt as the electric field.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p1:L18-L20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U03",
      "kind": "classification",
      "topic": "Conductors vs insulators",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "why",
        "comparison",
        "examples"
      ],
      "text": "Conductors allow current and are described as having free mobile electrons; insulators are described as not allowing current because they lack free mobile electrons.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p1:L25-L30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U04",
      "kind": "classification",
      "topic": "Static vs current electricity",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "comparison",
        "example"
      ],
      "text": "Static electricity is described as charge developing without flow; current electricity as charge in motion.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p1:L31-L34",
        "p2:L39-L40"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U05",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Open and closed circuit",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "when",
        "cause_effect"
      ],
      "text": "An open circuit has a broken contact so current does not flow; a closed circuit provides a continuous path.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p2:L40-L45"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U06",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Electric current",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "formula",
        "unit"
      ],
      "text": "Electric current is rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t; unit ampere; 1 A = 1 C/s.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p2:L46-L50"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U07",
      "kind": "instrument",
      "topic": "Ammeter",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "where"
      ],
      "text": "An ammeter measures current and is connected in series; the source explains this using its low resistance and constant series current.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p2:L49-L50"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U08",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Potential difference",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "formula",
        "unit"
      ],
      "text": "Potential difference is described as work done in moving charge between two specified points: V = W/q; one volt corresponds to 1 J per C.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p3:L58-L63"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U09",
      "kind": "instrument",
      "topic": "Voltmeter",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "where",
        "why"
      ],
      "text": "A voltmeter measures potential difference and is connected in parallel; the source attributes this to high resistance.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p3:L62-L65"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U10",
      "kind": "law",
      "topic": "Ohm's law",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "when",
        "relationship",
        "formula"
      ],
      "text": "At constant temperature, current is stated to be directly proportional to applied potential difference: V = IR.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p3:L66-L71"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U11",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Resistance",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "formula",
        "unit"
      ],
      "text": "Resistance is described as obstruction to charge flow: R = V/I; 1 ohm permits 1 A for 1 V.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p3:L72-L75"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U12",
      "kind": "relationship",
      "topic": "Factors affecting resistance",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "why",
        "how"
      ],
      "text": "The source relates resistance to length, cross-sectional area, material, and temperature, with longer wires and higher temperature described as increasing resistance and larger area decreasing it.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p3:L76-L81"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U13",
      "kind": "derivation",
      "topic": "Resistivity",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "formula",
        "unit"
      ],
      "text": "Combining resistance factors gives R = rho L/A; resistivity is described as a material property and its SI unit as ohm-metre.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p4:L86-L90"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U14",
      "kind": "comparison",
      "topic": "Series and parallel resistance",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "comparison",
        "formula",
        "application"
      ],
      "text": "Series: current remains constant and R = R1 + R2 + ...; parallel: potential difference remains constant and reciprocal resistances add. Household circuits are stated to be parallel.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p4:L91-L99"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U15",
      "kind": "instrument",
      "topic": "Rheostat and galvanometer",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "use"
      ],
      "text": "A rheostat is variable resistance used to change current without changing the voltage source; a galvanometer measures very small current.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p4:L100-L102"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U16",
      "kind": "law",
      "topic": "Joule heating",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "why",
        "how",
        "formula",
        "application"
      ],
      "text": "The source attributes heat production to collisions of moving electrons and gives H = I²RT; heating applications include iron, bulb, toaster and heater.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p4:L103-L104",
        "p5:L109-L114"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U17",
      "kind": "application",
      "topic": "Fuse and electrical safety",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "why",
        "how",
        "cause_effect"
      ],
      "text": "A fuse interrupts excessive current; high resistivity and low melting point allow overheating to melt the fuse. Overloading and short circuiting are identified as causes of excessive current.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p5:L115-L123"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U18",
      "kind": "safety",
      "topic": "Earthing",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "why",
        "how",
        "application"
      ],
      "text": "Earthing connects an appliance's metallic body to earth through a thick copper wire; the earth is described as 0 potential and the earth wire as a safety wire.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p5:L124-L131"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U19",
      "kind": "comparison",
      "topic": "DC vs AC",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "comparison",
        "application"
      ],
      "text": "DC is described as one-directional; AC periodically reverses direction and changes magnitude with time. The source states AC has advantages for long-distance transmission.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p6:L136-L138"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U20",
      "kind": "device",
      "topic": "Rectifier, inverter, transformer",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "conversion"
      ],
      "text": "Rectifier converts AC to DC; inverter converts DC to AC; transformer transfers AC electrical energy while stepping voltage up or down.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p6:L139-L144"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U21",
      "kind": "concept",
      "topic": "Magnetic effect of current",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "cause_effect"
      ],
      "text": "The source introduces magnetism produced by electricity and electricity produced by magnetism.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p6:L145-L145"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U22",
      "kind": "device",
      "topic": "Electromagnet",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "why",
        "factors"
      ],
      "text": "An electromagnet uses a current-carrying coil around a soft iron core and loses magnetism when current is switched off. Strength is linked to coil turns, current and pole-gap length.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p6:L146-L149"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U23",
      "kind": "comparison",
      "topic": "Soft iron vs steel in electromagnets",
      "question_types": [
        "why",
        "comparison",
        "application"
      ],
      "text": "The source says steel is unsuitable for an electromagnet because it retains magnetism after current is switched off.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p7:L154-L155"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U24",
      "kind": "comparison",
      "topic": "Electric motor vs generator",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "comparison",
        "energy_conversion"
      ],
      "text": "The page compares a motor converting electrical to mechanical energy with a generator converting mechanical to electrical energy; the image/text links the motor to force on a current-carrying conductor and the generator to electromagnetic induction.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p7 image",
        "p9:L213-L219"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U25",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Magnet and magnetic field",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "where",
        "properties"
      ],
      "text": "A magnet attracts iron/steel; a freely suspended magnet aligns north-south; like poles repel and unlike poles attract; magnets are described as dipoles; a magnetic field is the region where magnetic effect is felt.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p9:L219-L223"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U26",
      "kind": "definition",
      "topic": "Magnetic field lines",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "how",
        "why",
        "properties"
      ],
      "text": "The source describes field lines as originating at north and terminating at south outside the magnet, being closer where the field is stronger, not intersecting, and forming closed continuous curves.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p10:L228-L229"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U27",
      "kind": "assessment",
      "topic": "Numerical resistance problem",
      "question_types": [
        "application",
        "calculation"
      ],
      "text": "The source asks for resistance of an electric iron drawing 4 A at 220 V.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p7:L158-L162"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U28",
      "kind": "assessment",
      "topic": "Concept matching and statements",
      "question_types": [
        "what",
        "comparison",
        "classification"
      ],
      "text": "Pages 7-10 include MCQs, matching, statement questions and household AC/DC questions that test definitions, units, instruments, motor/generator, current direction and magnetic fields.",
      "source_refs": [
        "p7:L156-L174",
        "p8:L182-L205",
        "p9:L210-L223",
        "p10:L230-L247"
      ]
    }
  ],
